# João: capítulo 7 (definições comuns das experiências) e capítulo 8 (resultados dos cenários já corridos).
# Números de data/processed/kpis_choque_*.xlsx e resultados_cenarios.xlsx (scripts/kpis_choque.py e resultados_cenarios.py).

$content['Experiments and Simulation Scenarios'] = {
    Body 'All experiments use the calibrated model of Section 6.3 with its parameters fixed. Each run covers 120 trading days of the real information of 2023–2024, from 13 June to 27 November 2024 (days 369 to 488 of the shock file, initial price 0.3494 EUR, the real close of 12 June 2024). In the shock scenarios a negative information shock of -10% hits the fundamental value at minute 30 600, the start of day 60 (5 September 2024). This date was chosen before running any scenario by a fixed rule: no BCP announcement or Portuguese event in the 5 days before and the 10 days after the shock, and, among the 28 days that satisfy this condition, the one with the lowest volatility of BCP and of the market around it (BCP volatility of 0.95% per day in the 20 previous days, half of its usual level). In this way the effect of the collars is not mixed with real news, while the rest of the market keeps its real behaviour.'
    Body 'Each configuration is replicated 30 times. Replication k of every configuration uses the same random seed (1000 + k), so configurations are compared on the same sequences of random events and differ only in the parameters under study. The replications must be run one at a time: when they were run in parallel (first baseline run), they wrote to the same output file at the same time and about 1% of the lines were damaged; the baseline was therefore repeated.'
    Body 'As for the stylised facts, the indicators of the scenarios were fixed and committed to the project repository before any scenario was run (docs/KPIS_CENARIOS.md, 25 November 2026), so that they could not be adjusted to the results ({T:kpiDefs}). There is no regulatory definition of a crash. A crash is defined with the data of BCP as a maximum fall on the day of the shock larger than the information shock (-10%) plus the intraday fall that BCP exceeded on only 1% of the days of 2023–2024 (7.44%, from the daily low relative to the previous close), that is, a fall below -17.44%; the 95% block-bootstrap interval of this threshold is -19.60% to -15.21%. As a robustness check the probability of a crash is also computed with the 95th percentile (-14.52%), and the excess fall of each run is reported, which does not depend on any threshold. A conclusion on the probability of a crash is considered robust only if it has the same direction with both thresholds and with the excess fall.'
    Tbl 'Indicators of the scenarios, fixed before the runs' @('Group', 'Indicator', 'Definition') @(
        @('Stability', 'Maximum fall', 'Lowest price on the day of the shock relative to the price just before the shock'),
        @('Stability', 'Excess fall', 'Maximum fall minus the -10% information shock'),
        @('Stability', 'Probability of a crash', 'Share of the 30 runs with a maximum fall below -17.44% (robustness: -14.52%), with a 95% Wilson interval'),
        @('Stability', 'Close and 5-day return', 'Return from the price before the shock to the close of the shock day and of the 5th day after'),
        @('Stability', 'Half-recovery time', 'Minutes from the minimum until the price recovers half of the fall (within 5 days)'),
        @('Liquidity', 'Spread', 'Mean relative spread outside reservations, on the shock day and in the 5 following days'),
        @('Liquidity', 'Volume', 'Shares traded on the shock day and in the 5 following days'),
        @('Liquidity', 'Time without continuous trading', 'Minutes in reservation and number of reservations on the shock day')
    ) @(2.4, 4.2, 9.4) 'kpiDefs'
    Body 'For each configuration the mean of each indicator is reported with its 95% confidence interval. Configurations with collars are compared with the same configuration without collars by the difference of the means, its 95% interval and a Welch t-test (unequal variances); the number of crashes is compared with Fisher exact test. The multi-criteria analysis normalises each indicator between 0 and 1 over all collar configurations, averages the stability indicators (maximum fall, probability of a crash, half-recovery time) and the liquidity indicators (spread, volume, time in reservation), and weights stability and liquidity 50/50, with 70/30 and 30/70 as robustness checks; a configuration is recommended only if it ranks first with the three weights.'
}

$content['Large Shock with and without Collars: Results'] = {
    Body 'With 13 trend followers, the calibrated share of the model, the -10% shock produces a maximum fall of about -15.5% on the day of the shock, that is, about 5.5 points more than the information justifies, and the price closes the day about 10.8% lower, in line with the new fundamental value ({T:res2}). The current collars of Euronext Lisbon (3% dynamic, 8% static) stop continuous trading for about 10 minutes on the day of the shock but hardly change the outcome: the maximum fall is 0.3 points smaller, a difference that is not statistically significant (p = 0.38), and the number of crashes goes from 3 to 2 out of 30 (p = 1.0). The spread and the traded volume are the same with and without collars.'
    Tbl 'Large shock (-10%) with and without the current collars, 13 trend followers (30 runs each)' @('Indicator', 'Without collars', 'With collars', 'Difference [95% CI]', 'p') @(
        @('Maximum fall on the shock day', '-15.75%', '-15.42%', '+0.33 pp [-0.4, +1.1]', '0.38'),
        @('Crashes (below -17.44%)', '3 / 30', '2 / 30', '-', '1.00 (Fisher)'),
        @('Crashes, robustness (below -14.52%)', '25 / 30', '25 / 30', '-', '1.00 (Fisher)'),
        @('Return at the close of the shock day', '-10.75%', '-10.85%', '-0.10 pp', '0.80'),
        @('Minutes in reservation on the shock day', '0', '10.3', '+10.3', '< 0.001'),
        @('Spread on the shock day', '0.11%', '0.11%', '0.00 pp', '0.79'),
        @('Volume on the shock day (million shares)', '4.80', '4.75', '-1.2%', '0.82')
    ) @(5.4, 2.4, 2.4, 3.6, 2.2) 'res2'
    Body 'The half-recovery time is not informative in this scenario. It measures the recovery of half of the total fall, which includes the -10% of the news: that part is justified by the information and permanent, so the price only gets back above -7.9% by chance, after one to three days on average (1 512 and 1 299 minutes, difference not significant, p = 0.51). This is a limitation of how the indicator was defined before the runs; it is kept as defined and interpreted with this caveat.'
}

$content['Shock with a High Share of Trend Followers: Results'] = {
    Body 'With 60 trend followers and herding (a trend follower panics after receiving between 1 and 4 sell signals from its neighbours, herdThreshold), the same -10% shock turns into a crash in most runs: without collars the maximum fall is between -21% and -27% and between 80% and 100% of the runs fall below -17.44%, against 10% with 13 trend followers ({T:res3}). The share of trend followers is therefore the main driver of the probability and size of crashes (research question 1). The effect of the herding threshold is not monotonic: the smallest fall occurs with the most sensitive herding (one signal), possibly because panic is triggered earlier and spread over time; the separate roles of the number of trend followers and of herding are analysed with the grid of collar configurations, reservation durations and numbers of trend followers (Collar Thresholds and Reservation Duration).'
    Tbl 'Shock with 60 trend followers and herding, with and without the current collars (30 runs each)' @('Sell signals for panic', 'Maximum fall without / with collars', 'Difference [95% CI], p', 'Crashes without / with collars', 'Minutes in reservation with collars') @(
        @('1', '-21.1% / -19.4%', '+1.7 pp [-0.4, +3.7], p = 0.11', '24 / 17 of 30 (p = 0.10)', '45'),
        @('2', '-26.8% / -22.1%', '+4.7 pp [+2.7, +6.8], p < 0.001', '30 / 24 of 30 (p = 0.02)', '72'),
        @('3', '-26.0% / -22.7%', '+3.3 pp [+1.3, +5.2], p = 0.001', '29 / 29 of 30 (p = 1.0)', '94'),
        @('4', '-24.7% / -22.2%', '+2.5 pp [+0.1, +4.9], p = 0.04', '27 / 26 of 30 (p = 1.0)', '97')
    ) @(2.6, 3.4, 4.0, 3.4, 2.6) 'res3'
    Body 'In this market the collars matter. They reduce the maximum fall by 2.5 to 4.7 points when two or more sell signals are needed for panic (p < 0.05), at the cost of 72 to 97 minutes without continuous trading on the day of the shock. The reduction in the probability of a crash is significant only with two signals and the main threshold (from 30 to 24 runs out of 30, p = 0.02); with the robustness threshold it disappears (30 and 29 runs), so by the rule fixed before the runs it is not a robust result. The spread and the volume do not differ significantly between the two cases. In every configuration the return at the close of the shock day is the same with and without collars (p > 0.5): the collars cut the excess fall caused by panic within the day, but they do not change the level at which the price settles, which reflects the information. Because the reservations stop trading, the half-recovery time is longer with collars (by 6 to 12 minutes, p = 0.008 to 0.05 with two to four signals).'
}

$content['Baseline Scenario: Results'] = {
    Body 'The baseline runs the same 120 days without the large shock and serves as the reference for the shock scenarios. On the reference day (day 60, 5 September 2024) the largest intraday fall is 1.1% on average, no run comes close to the crash thresholds (0 out of 30 with both thresholds), the price closes 0.9% higher, the relative spread is 0.10% and 4.5 million shares are traded; with the current collars there is no reservation on that day. These values describe an ordinary trading day of the model and confirm that the crashes of the shock scenarios are caused by the shock and the reaction of the agents, not by the background information. The baseline was also used for the one-minute comparison with BCP (Section 6.3).'
}

$content['Optimal Thresholds: Results'] = {
    Body 'The thresholds of the collars were varied in two markets: the calibrated market with 13 trend followers (dynamic threshold 2% to 5% and static threshold 6% to 12%, 16 combinations) and the market with 60 trend followers and herding (two sell signals for panic), where the collars had a significant effect (static threshold 6% to 12%, dynamic threshold fixed at 3%). In both cases each combination is compared with the same market without collars and the same random seeds. In the calibrated market the dynamic threshold had no effect at all: for a given static threshold the results are practically identical with a dynamic threshold of 2% or 5%. The dynamic collar compares each trade with the previous one, and in the model the price falls through many small steps after the shock, so no single trade moves the price by 2% or more. Only the static collar, measured against the reference price, stops the fall.'
    Body 'In the calibrated market no combination changes the maximum fall significantly (between -15.2% and -16.0%, against -15.8% without collars; all p > 0.1) or the number of crashes (p >= 0.24); the only significant effect is the cost, from 3 minutes in reservation with a static threshold of 12% to 16 minutes with 6%. The multi-criteria analysis finds no configuration that ranks first with the three weights, because it orders differences that are within the noise of the replications. In a market like the calibrated one the choice of the thresholds therefore matters little.'
    Body 'With herding the static threshold matters and shows a clear trade-off ({T:resThr}): the tighter the threshold, the smaller the fall and the longer the market is stopped. All four thresholds reduce the maximum fall significantly (by 3.8 to 6.6 points, p < 0.001), and the 6% and 8% thresholds also reduce the number of crashes with the main threshold (p = 0.01 and 0.02), although not with the robustness threshold. The spread is slightly lower with collars (0.134% to 0.139% against 0.141%) and the volume does not differ significantly. The return at the close of the shock day does not differ significantly from the case without collars in any configuration.'
    Tbl 'Static collar threshold in the market with herding (60 trend followers, two sell signals; dynamic threshold 3%; 30 runs each)' @('Static threshold', 'Maximum fall', 'Difference to no collars [95% CI]', 'Crashes (-17.44%)', 'Minutes in reservation', 'Rank 50/50, 70/30, 30/70') @(
        @('No collars', '-26.8%', '-', '30 / 30', '0', '5, 5, 5'),
        @('6%', '-20.2%', '+6.6 pp [+4.8, +8.5]', '23 / 30', '115', '1, 1, 1'),
        @('8% (current)', '-22.1%', '+4.7 pp [+2.8, +6.7]', '24 / 30', '72', '2, 2, 3'),
        @('10%', '-23.0%', '+3.8 pp [+2.0, +5.5]', '29 / 30', '45', '3, 3, 4'),
        @('12%', '-22.6%', '+4.2 pp [+2.3, +6.0]', '26 / 30', '29', '4, 4, 2')
    ) @(2.4, 2.2, 3.6, 2.4, 2.4, 3.0) 'resThr'
    Body 'In this market the static threshold of 6% ranks first with the three weights of the multi-criteria analysis, so by the rule fixed before the runs it is the recommended configuration, and having no collars ranks last with every weight. This ranking should be read with care. The liquidity criterion averages three indicators normalised between 0 and 1, and the 6% threshold has the best spread and the highest volume although the differences are small (0.007 points of spread) and the difference in volume is not significant, while it has by far the longest time without continuous trading (almost two hours on the day of the shock). A decision maker who gives more weight to the time without trading than the equal weights of the analysis would prefer a wider threshold: 12% keeps about two thirds of the reduction of the fall obtained with 6% with a quarter of the time in reservation. The trade-off in {T:resThr} is therefore the main result for the decision, and the multi-criteria ranking one way of reading it.'
}

$content['Sensitivity Analysis: Results'] = {
    Body 'To check whether the effect of the collars depends on the parameters that could not be observed, the large-shock scenario was repeated with each of the most influential parameters identified in Section 6.3 at -50% and +50% of its calibrated value, one at a time, with and without the current collars ({T:resSens}). The results for the order rate of the noise traders (noiseRate) are not yet available.'
    Tbl 'Sensitivity of the effect of the current collars (13 trend followers, -10% shock, 30 runs each)' @('Parameter', 'Value', 'Maximum fall without / with collars', 'Difference, p', 'Crashes without / with collars') @(
        @('Calibrated values', '-', '-15.8% / -15.4%', '+0.3 pp, 0.38', '3 / 2'),
        @('Order lifetime (orderLife)', '17.5 min', '-16.3% / -15.9%', '+0.4 pp, 0.39', '10 / 5'),
        @('', '52.5 min', '-15.5% / -15.4%', '+0.1 pp, 0.78', '3 / 3'),
        @('Number of trend followers', '7', '-13.4% / -13.5%', '-0.1 pp, 0.72', '0 / 0'),
        @('', '20', '-18.3% / -17.5%', '+0.7 pp, 0.08', '21 / 17'),
        @('Trend threshold (trendThreshold)', '0.25%', '-16.3% / -16.4%', '0.0 pp, 0.96', '10 / 8'),
        @('', '0.75%', '-15.6% / -15.6%', '0.0 pp, 0.96', '4 / 3'),
        @('Number of noise traders', '15', '-17.3% / -17.0%', '+0.3 pp, 0.52', '16 / 11'),
        @('', '45', '-14.5% / -14.1%', '+0.4 pp, 0.35', '2 / 1')
    ) @(4.4, 1.8, 3.8, 2.8, 3.2) 'resSens'
    Body 'The effect of the collars does not change sign in any case: it is small and not significant, between -0.1 and +0.7 points, as with the calibrated values, so the conclusion for the calibrated market does not depend on these parameters. What changes strongly is the risk of a crash itself. With 20 trend followers instead of 13, 21 of the 30 runs without collars fall below -17.44%, and with 15 noise traders instead of 30 (a less liquid market) 16 of the 30 runs do; with 7 trend followers or 45 noise traders crashes almost disappear. This confirms that the share of trend followers and the liquidity provided by other investors are the main drivers of crashes in the model (research question 1). The sensitivity analysis was run in the calibrated market; whether the larger effect of the collars under herding is equally robust to these parameters was not tested within the project.'
}
